---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Chain from "../../components/Chain.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { tokens } from "../../lib/tokens";

const counts = {
  primitive: tokens.filter((t) => t.tier === "primitive").length,
  semantic: tokens.filter((t) => t.tier === "semantic").length,
  component: tokens.filter((t) => t.tier === "component").length,
};

const badExample = `/* Esto build fails. */
.sk-button {
  --sk-button-bg: var(--ramp-accent-600);
}`;

const linterOutput = `✗ 1 problem(s):

  [tier-skip]
    --sk-button-bg: component hook reaches past the semantic
    layer into primitive --ramp-accent-600, bypasses mode
    switching, breaks dark mode for this component alone`;
---

<Base
  title="Tres tiers"
  description="Primitives, semantics and styling hooks: what each tier can reference, and why the direction of the references is what makes it a system."
>
  <h1>Three tiers</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A tier is not defined by the folder where it lives, but by <strong>which</strong> can reference. Three folders called primitive/semantic/component do not make a three-tier system; makes it the obligatory address of references.
  </p>

  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Tier</th>
          <th>Responde</th>
          <th>You can reference</th>
          <th>How many are there</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="sk-badge" data-tone="warning">primitive</span></td>
          <td>what values exist</td>
          <td>nothing, is the background</td>
          <td class="mono">{counts.primitive}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="sk-badge" data-tone="accent">semantic</span></td>
          <td>what does a value mean</td>
          <td>primitives, and other semantics</td>
          <td class="mono">{counts.semantic}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="sk-badge" data-tone="success">component</span></td>
          <td>where a value is used</td>
          <td>semantics only, <strong>never</strong> primitives</td>
          <td class="mono">{counts.component}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The string, live</h2>
  <p>
    Each rung shows its currently used value. Change the <strong>mark to</strong> above and see what moves: the literal below changes hue, and the names above do not notice. That indifference is the entire mechanism.
  </p>
  <p>
    The chain is split into two where there is a mode-aware token, because <code>light-dark()</code> has two parents. Change the <strong>mode to</strong> and you will see which of the two slots is using your value above.
  </p>

  <Chain
    token="--sk-button-bg"
    caption="The background of a button, from the styling hook to the literal."
  />

  <Chain
    token="--color-action-primary"
    caption="A semantic token names a purpose and points to a ramp position, never a hue."
  />

  <h2>Why references only go down</h2>
  <p>
    A semanticist can point to a primitive or sideways to another semanticist. A styling hook can point to a semantic hook. Nothing points up, and tier 3 <strong>cannot jump to tier 1</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That jump is the one that seems harmless and is not. A styling hook that takes <code>--ramp-accent-600</code> directly skips the layer where <code>light-dark()</code> lives. That component, and only that one, no longer has a dark mode. The defect is not seen until someone looks at the page in darkness.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock code={badExample} label="components/button.css" note="tier 3 → tier 1" />
  <CodeBlock code={linterOutput} label="pnpm lint" note="the build crashes" />

  <div class="sk-alert" data-tone="info" data-presentation="accent">
    <div class="sk-alert__content">
    <p>
      It is not a documented convention: it is an executable rule. The validator runs over the CSS before each build, in <em>all</em> marks, because a rule that is only satisfied in the default palette is not a rule. The output above is real: it comes from aiming a styling hook at a ramp on purpose.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The prefix marks the tier</h2>
  <p>
    A primitive reads <code>--ramp-accent-600</code>; a semantic, <code>--color-action-primary</code>. The different prefix is intentional: seeing a <code>--ramp-*</code> within the CSS of a component is the visible sign, at first glance, that someone has skipped a tier. Flattening <code>--accent-600</code> would lose that signal.
  </p>

  <h2>The edge of the tier is a cascade layer</h2>
  <p>
    The precedence between tiers does not depend on the order of the <code>@import</code>: it is in the cascade. A later layer wins, so mark and contrast overrides always beat base semantics, regardless of import order.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`@layer primitives, semantic, components, overrides;`}
    label="tokens.scss"
    note="the order that decides everything"
  />
  <p>
    And it has a benefit: since <strong>any rule without a layer beats all layers</strong>, your app can step on a styling hook without fighting specificity.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={`.hero .sk-button { --sk-button-bg: rebeccapurple; }`} label="CSS" />
</Base>
